Let $(X_i, Y_i)$, $i = 1, \dots, n$, be independent and identically distributed bivariate random pairs whose component-wise minima, after appropriate normalization to reduced exponential margins, have joint survival function
$$S(x,y) = \exp\{-(x+y)\,A\!\left(\tfrac{x}{x+y}\right)\}, \qquad x,y > 0,$$
where the dependence function $A:[0,1] \to [1/2,1]$ satisfies
(i) $A(0) = A(1) = 1$;
(ii) $0 \le -A'(0)$ and $A'(1) < 1$;
(iii) $A$ is convex on $[0,1]$;
(iv) $\max(u,1-u) \le A(u) \le 1$ for all $u \in [0,1]$.

Equivalently, under the transformation linking reduced Gumbel maxima to reduced exponential minima, the corresponding bivariate extreme-value distribution function may be written as
$$F(x,y) = \exp\{-(e^{-x} + e^{-y})\,k(y-x)\},$$
where $k$ is the associated dependence function in the Gumbel parametrization.

Classical intrinsic (nonparametric) estimators of $A$, such as those of Pickands and of Tiago de Oliveira, produce estimators $\widehat{A}_n$ that are not twice differentiable; consequently the induced estimated survival function $\widehat{S}_n(x,y) = \exp\{-(x+y)\widehat{A}_n(x/(x+y))\}$ (and the corresponding distribution function in the Gumbel form) does not possess a density with respect to Lebesgue measure on the plane.

Problem. Construct an intrinsic (nonparametric) estimator $\widehat{A}_n$ of $A$ (equivalently $\widehat{k}_n$ of $k$) based on the sample $(X_i, Y_i)_{i=1}^n$ such that, with probability one (or at least for all sufficiently large $n$),
(a) $\widehat{A}_n$ is a valid dependence function, i.e. it satisfies (i)–(iv) above;
(b) $\widehat{A}_n \in C^2([0,1])$;
so that the induced bivariate extreme-value model admits a planar density and enjoys improved analytic tractability. Establish the consistency and asymptotic distribution of such an estimator.

